import
	../../html
	../../page-template

tutorial-index html[]()
	- () section
		- () p quote
			In this section, we'll run this "hello world" program using different methods.
		- () crow-runnable quote
			main void()
				info log "Hello, world!"
		- "Run in the browser" h2
		- () p quote
			Click the {"play" crow-icon} button at the bottom of the code block above to run it in the browser.
			{br}
			The code is editable, so you can test things out there while reading the tutorial.
			{br}
			This supports all Crow standard library functions, but it can't do I/O except for printing.

		- "Run from the command line" h2
		- () p quote
			For the next step, you must {"download.html" link "download"} and install crow.
			{br}
			Copy the example above and save to a file {"hello.crow" code}.
			{br}
			Then, run {"crow hello.crow" code}.
		- () p quote
			The longer form is {"crow run hello.crow" code}. This allows passing additional arguments like {"--watch" code}.

		- "Script arguments" h3
		- () p quote
			If you use {"crow hello.crow foo" code}, {"foo" code} becomes the first command-line argument to the script.
			{br}
			If you use {"crow run hello.crow foo" code}, {"foo" code} is treated as an argument to {"crow" code} instead (producing an error).
			{br}
			To work around this, command-line arguments to {"crow run" code} should come after a {"--" code}, as in {"crow run hello.crow -- foo" code}.

		- "Watch mode" h3
		- () p quote
			You can also use {"crow run hello.crow --watch" code} to re-run the script every time it (or its imports) changes.

		- "Build" h3
		- () p quote
			Run {"crow build hello.crow" code} to generate an executable JAR file.
			{br}
			You can then run {"./hello" code} to run it.
			{br}
			On some systems you must run {"java -jar hello" code}.
		- () p quote
			You can also run {"crow build hello.crow --out hello.js" code} to generate JavaScript.
			{br}
			The result can be loaded as a script tag in a browser using {"<script type=\"module\" src=\"/hello.js\"></script>" code}.
			{br}
			It can also be run with {"node hello.js" code}. This isn't really an intended use case, but may be more convenient for testing.
		- () p "{"crow build hello.crow --watch"} will rebuild the executable every time something changes."

		- "Compile errors don't stop the program" h3
		- () p quote
			For convenience, compile errors don't always prevent the program from starting.
			This allows you to use the parts of your program that don't have compile errors.
			{br}
			Instead, they compile to code that throws an exception.
			The below example will log {"hello, world" code}, then throw an exception:
		- () crow-runnable quote
			main void()
				info log "hello, world"
				function-that-does-not-exist
				info log "goodbye, world"
